# Terminal server routing table.
#
#   regex            host             port
#
# Three whitespace-separated columns. Whitespace is the delimiter because
# regexes routinely contain commas -- [0-9]{2,3} -- and colons, but almost
# never spaces. Everything after a # is a comment.
#
# The regex is matched with re.fullmatch against the user part of BOTH the
# Request-URI and the To: header of the inbound INVITE. Rules are tried in
# file order and the first rule matching either number wins, so put the
# specific ones first and any catch-all last.
#
# fullmatch, not search: 620 does not match **6201. A prefix rule is 620.*
# and a suffix rule is .*620.
#
# A number matching no rule is rejected with 404 Not Found. A malformed line
# is a startup error rather than a silently skipped rule.
#
# Which number is "the dialled number" depends on your switch, and it is worth
# checking rather than assuming: run termsrv.py --dump-invite and look. A
# FRITZ!Box rewrites the Request-URI and To to the registered account name and
# puts the dialled extension only in P-Called-Party-ID, so on this rig every
# call would otherwise route as the account name no matter what was dialled.

\*\*9              127.0.0.1        2323
\*\*62[0-9]        127.0.0.1        2323
\*\*7[0-9]{2,3}    127.0.0.1        23

# A catch-all, if you want one. Without it, unrouted numbers get a 404.
# .*               127.0.0.1        2000
